<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Taron</h1><p class="st-meta">826 CE – 966 CE (approx.) · capital Mush</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Armenian principality west of Lake Van, ruled by a branch of the Bagratids, that passed peacefully to Byzantium in the 960s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Taron, the plain around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mus">Mush</a>, had long been a Mamikonian land. In the early 9th century the Bagratid Bagrat, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashot-msaker">Ashot Msaker</a>, secured it as his share of the family lands, and in 826 his branch became its hereditary princes under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Taron was a fertile, well-watered district on routes between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and its monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surb-karapet-monastery">Surb Karapet</a> at Glak was among the most revered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Its princes balanced between the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, the kings of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bagratid-armenia">Bagratid Armenia</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> describes Prince Grigor visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and receiving a palace and pension.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 966 Grigor's descendants surrendered Taron to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in return for court titles and estates. It became a Byzantine theme, the first of the Armenian principalities to be annexed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 826 CE – 966 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Caucasus</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:76.14%;width:2.79%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>826 CE – 966 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mush</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bagrat, Grigor</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Armenian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Taron, Bagratids of Taron</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=896&amp;focus=principality-of-taron" aria-label="Principality of Taron on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5558 771 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Taron lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5878 1027h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5878 1027h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Turkey. <a href="/atlas?year=896&amp;focus=principality-of-taron">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

